# Revealing Co‐N4@Co‐NP Bridge‐Enabled Fast Charge Transfer and Active Intracellular Methanogenesis in Bio‐Electrochemical CO2‐Conversion with Methanosarcina Barkeri

## abstract
To significantly advance the bio-electrochemical CO2 -conversion rate and unfold the correlation between the abiotic electrode and the attached microorganisms, an atomic-nanoparticle bridge of Co-N4 @Co-NP crafted in metal-organic frameworks-derived nanosheets is integrated with a model methanogen of Methanosarcina barkeri (M. barkeri). The direct bonding of N in Co-N4 and Fe in member protein of Cytochrome b (Cytb) activates a fast direct electron transfer path while the Co nanoparticles further strengthen this bonding via decreasing the energy gap between the p-band center of N and the d-band center of Fe. This multiorbital tuning operation of Co nanoparticles also enhances the coenzyme F420-mediated electron transfer by enabling the electron flow direct to the hydrogenation sites. Particularly, the increased surface electric field of the Co-N4 @Co-NP bridge-based nanosheet electrode facilitates the interfacial Na+ accumulation to expedite ATPase transport for powering intracellular CO2 conversion. Remarkably, the self-assembled M.barkeri-Co-N4 @Co-NP biohybrid achieves a high methane production rate of 3860 mmol m-2 day-1 , which greatly outperforms other reported biohybrid systems. This work demonstrates a comprehensive scrutinization of biotic-abiotic energy transfer, which may serve as a guiding principle for efficient bio-electrochemical system design.

Revealing Co-N4@Co-NP Bridge-Enabled Fast Charge Transfer and Active Intracellular Methanogenesis in Bio-Electrochemical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -Conversion with Methanosarcina Barkeri
To significantly advance the bio-electrochemical $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ -conversion rate and unfold the correlation between the abiotic electrode and the attached microorganisms, an atomic-nanoparticle bridge of Co- $N_{4}@{\mathsf{C o}}$ -NP crafted in metal–organic frameworks-derived nanosheets is integrated with a model methanogen of Methanosarcina barkeri (M. barkeri). The direct bonding of N in $\mathsf{C o-N}_{4}$ and Fe in member protein of Cytochrome b (Cytb) activates a fast direct electron transfer path while the Co nanoparticles further strengthen this bonding via decreasing the energy gap between the p-band center of N and the d-band center of Fe. This multiorbital tuning operation of Co nanoparticles also enhances the coenzyme F420-mediated electron transfer by enabling the electron flow direct to the hydrogenation sites. Particularly, the increased surface electric field of the $\mathsf{C o-N}_{4}@\mathsf{C o-N P}$ bridge-based nanosheet electrode facilitates the interfacial $\mathsf{N}\mathsf{a}^{+}$ accumulation to expedite ATPase transport for powering intracellular $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ conversion. Remarkably, the self-assembled M.barkeri-Co- $N_{4}@{\mathsf{C o}}$ -NP biohybrid achieves a high methane production rate of 3860 mmol $\pmb{m}^{-2}\mathbf{day}^{-1}$ , which greatly outperforms other reported biohybrid systems. This work demonstrates a comprehensive scrutinization of biotic–abiotic energy transfer, which may serve as a guiding principle for efficient bio-electrochemical system design.
## 1. Introduction
Electrochemical carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ reduction at room temperature contributes to achieving carbon neutrality and storing intermittent renewable electricity.[1–3] Methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ which has a high calorific value of 890 kJ $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ and can be integrated into the existing energy infrastructure, is a desired target product for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion.[4,5] Yet, the ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}–\mathrm{to}–{\mathrm{CH}}_{4}$ conversion is a kinetically complex process that requires exactly orchestrated activation energy within the multiple proton-coupled electron transfer steps.[6,7] For this reason, various homogenous and/or heterogenous catalysts are facing challenges related to low catalytic activity and product selectivity, as well as poor system stability in the electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to- $\mathrm{.CH_{4}}$ conversion process.[8–10] These issues might be well addressed by using biological methanogens as catalysts, during which methanogens sustainably harness reducing equivalents from the abiotic electrode matrix to realize effective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{to}{\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ conversion. The key to desired methanogenesis performance is to maximize the electron extraction ability of methanogens from the electrode matrix.[11–14] However, the improvement of this electron transfer process is largely constrained by the convoluted and unclear charge transfer pathways at the biotic–abiotic interface.[1,11,15] Therefore, to pave the way for improving the bioelectrochemical methanogenesis performance, it is imperative to unravel the specific microbial-extracellular electron transfer process through scrutinizing the interactions between methanogens and electrode matrix.
The metabolism rate of methanogens represents another critical factor that affects $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion speed. Intracellular methanogenesis is a complex multistep reaction yet its activity can be simply evaluated by adenosine triphosphate (ATP) synthesis since the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to- $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ conversion is an energetically intensive process.[16,17] It is worth pointing out that ATP synthesis by ATPase is mainly driven by the $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ concentration in the synthetic medium. A higher $\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ concentration around the cell provides a larger driving force for ATP synthesis.[18] As the methanogens are tightly attached to the electrode matrix to receive reducing equivalents in bio-electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion, their ATP synthesis thus relies on the local $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ concentration near the bio-electrode. Generally, interfacial ion transfer and distribution are largely affected by the microenvironment, including both electronic and architectural structures of the electrode, which also are well-known contributing factors for biointerfacial charge transfer.[19–22] However, in sharp contrast to copious work on biotic–abiotic interface engineering for improved charge transfer, the study on exploiting the structural effect of the electrode on the metabolism of methanogens, and by extension on the bio-electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate, has been comparatively neglected.
The revelation of the charge transfer mechanisms at the biotic– abiotic interface necessitates the identification of interaction sites and electron acceptors. The Co-based atomic-nanoparticle bridge $(\mathrm{Co-N}_{4}@\mathrm{Co-NP})$ developed in our previous study provides a robust platform to investigate the bio-interfacial electrointeraction as the atomic ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ sites act as the target interaction sites and the underneath Co nanoparticles afford further convenient modification of the interfacial interaction.[23] Yet, the adoption of a mixed microbiome in that work complicates the microbial electron receptors, making it impracticable to scrutinize the pivotal role of ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ electrode in promoting the extracellular electron transfer and intracellular methanogenesis for specific methanogens.[23] Herein, a model methanogen Methanosarcina barkeri (M. barkeri) is selected to combine with the efficient Co $\mathbf{\partial}_{-N_{4}@C o-N P}$ bridge-based nanosheet electrode for systematically interrogating the charge transfer mechanisms at the biotic–abiotic interface and the structural effects of the electrode on intracellular metabolism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{to}{\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Interfacial direct electron transfer pathway from electrode matrix to iron porphyrin (Fe-TCPP), which is the center molecule of conductive cytochrome $b$ (Cytb), as well as mediated electron transfer via the redox reaction of riboflavin—the center molecule of coenzyme $F_{420}^{\mathrm{~}}.$ —were carefully interrogated at different applied potentials to reveal the function of ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}$ sites and the underneath Co nanoparticles. Particularly, the structure-dependent electric field of the electrode was studied via finite element analysis (FEA) to demonstrate its effect on manipulating intracellular metabolism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion through tailoring the interfacial $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ distribution. Such systemic scrutinization of the biotic–abiotic interface in terms of both electron transfer and intracellular metabolism may bring new insights into the design of a wide range of electrochemical systems for different applications.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Construction of M. barkeri-Co- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}@C o-N P}$ Biohybrid
The synthetic procedure of the M. barkeri-Co- ${\bf\cdot N}_{4}@{\bf C o-N P}$ biohybrid is shown in Figure 1a. ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-based nanosheet matrix was prepared according to our previous study.[23] First, Co-doped zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (ZIF67) was synthesized in situ on carbon felt (CF) to form a stable nanosheet-based structure (Figure S1, Supporting Information), which is supposed to facilitate microbe enrichment during biohybrid construction. The as-prepared frameworks were then subjected to pyrolysis to form atomic ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ sites (Figure 1b) within carbonized ZIF-67, during which the excess Co species precipitated and agglomerated under the nanosheets as Co nanoparticles (Figure 1c), leading to the formation of Co- $\mathrm{N}_{4}@\mathrm{Co}.$ - NP bridge-based nanosheet electrode. The successful constructions of ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}$ coordination and the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge were confirmed with K-edge X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES, Figure S2, Supporting Information and Figure 1d), Fourier transform extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS, Figure S3, Supporting Information), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Figure S4, Supporting Information), and scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM)-energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping (Figure S5, Supporting Information).
The M. barkeri-Co- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}@C o-N P}$ biohybrid was developed via a stepped start-up process with ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge as the working electrode using an H-type electrolytic cell (see the Experimental Section in the Supporting Information). For comparison, a control biohybrid composed of M. barkeri-CF was also constructed via the same method. The scanning electron microscope (SEM)-EDS images (Figure S6, Supporting Information) of the M. barkeri-Co- $\mathrm{\bfN}_{4}@\mathrm{Co-NP}$ biohybrid suggest that the active elements maintained their dispersion without extensive agglomeration or splitting by decomposition. Notably, compared to the bare CF electrode, a significantly increased number of microbes is found on the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge (Figure 1f,g). The activity of the attached microbes on the as-prepared biohybrids was further characterized by assessing confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM) images of LIVE-DEAD stained assays (Figure 1h,i), in which the green and red fluorescence represents the living cells and dead cells, respectively.[24] The living-cells biofilm derived on the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-based nanosheets $(\approx122.6\ \mathrm{mm})$ is much thicker than that on bare CF $(\approx64.2~\mathrm{mm}$ . Furthermore, the total biomass concentration of the M. barkeri-Co- $\cdot\mathrm{N}_{4}@\mathrm{Co}.$ NP biohybrid achieves a 3.9-fold increase compared to that of the M. barkeri-CF based on quantitative DNA extraction tests. This higher biocompatibility of the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-based nanosheet electrode than the bare CF electrode can be attributed to two aspects, i.e., the introduction of the nanosheet arrays and the Co-based active sites. Straightforwardly, the nanosheets provide more attachment sites compared to CF. Meanwhile, the electrostatic interactions between the microbes and the electrode matrix are enhanced because of the increased surface electric intensity resulting from Co species.[23] As depicted in Figure 1e, the X-ray absorption spectrum (XAS) of Co shows that after the loading of M. barkeri, the absorption near edge of the Co species demonstrates a shift from Co foil toward phthalocyanine-cobalt (Co-Pc), suggesting an increased average valence state of the Co species resulting from the corresponding charge migration from Co element. Besides, the EXAFS spectra show a shift of $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ coordination from 1.7 to $1.8\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ with M. barkeri adsorption, indicating the distortion of the $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ coordination (Figure S7, Supporting Information). This implies that apart from the function of surface electronegativity tuning, the exterior $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ sites also act as the key active sites for electronic communication with the attached microbes.
## 2.2. Outstanding $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ -Conversion Performance of M. barkeri-Co-N4@Co-NP Biohybrid
Methanogenesis experiments were conducted in a typical H-type cell with M. barkeri-Co ${\bf\cdot N_{4}@C o-N P}$ working as the bio-cathode for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion (Figure 2a). Before each trial, the cell chambers were refilled with new growth medium, and the headspace of the cathode chamber was flushed with $99.999\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ as the only carbon source for methane production. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction performance was tested at various potentials from $-0.8$ to $-1.1\mathrm{V}$ versus $\sf A g/A g C l$ (Figure 2b,c). In sharp contrast to the control M. barkeri-CF biohybrid, dramatically higher methane yields with larger faradaic efficiency (FE) are detected at all potentials with M. barkeri-Co- $\mathrm{\cdotN}_{4}@$ Co-NP biohybrid. This suggests that the ${\bf C o-N_{4}@C o-N P}$ bridge-based electrode with better biocompatibility can effectively transfer the reducing equivalents to M. barkeri for methanogenesis. To verify that all methane productions strictly relied on the supplied electrons from the abiotic electrode, the circuits were cut off after the testing period, and no further methane production was detected. The highest methane production rate of 3868 mmol $\mathrm{m}^{-2}~\mathrm{day}^{-1}$ is obtained at $-1.0\mathrm{V}$ versus $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ (i.e., $-0.387\mathrm{V}$ vs standard hydrogen electrode) with $M$ . barkeri-Co- ${\bf\cdot N}_{4}@{\bf C o-N P}$ , which is ${\approx}13.5$ -fold higher than that with M. barkeri-CF. Notably, this methane yield is also superior to reported biotic–abiotic electrochemical systems at the same applied potential (Figure 2h and Table S4, Supporting Information).[25–31] Besides, the M. barkeri-Co $\mathbf{\partial}_{-N_{4}}\textcircled{a}\mathbf{Co-NP}$ also demonstrates good stability as indicated by the linear increase of the methane production volume under continuous operation for $72\mathrm{{h}}$ (Figure 2d).
To elucidate the excellent performance of the M. barkeri-Co${\bf N}_{4}@{\bf C o-N P}$ biohybrid for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion, simple yet robust electrochemical characteristics were first carried out (Figure 2e–g).
The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) of the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-based nanosheet electrode demonstrates greatly increased current density compared to the bare CF electrode, suggesting the introduction of the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-based nanosheet on the CF significantly increases its electrochemical performance (Figure 2e). Besides, a higher current density is further observed after the M. barkeri-Co- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}@C o-N P}$ biohybrid was established, indicating an efficient electron transfer from the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-based nanosheets to the $M$ barkeri for methanogenesis. The larger current density obtained with M. barkeri-Co- $\mathrm{\bfN}_{4}@\mathrm{Co}.$ NP biohybrid is corresponding to a smaller electrochemical resistance, as depicted in Figure 2g. The obtained Nyquist plots are modeled with the Randles equivalent circuit, including a series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ , a biofilm resistance $(R_{\mathrm{bio}})$ , and extracellular electron transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ . Notably, an impressively decreased $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ , i.e., the low-frequency response arc, is obtained with M. barkeri-C $\mathrm{\Lambda_{>-N_{4}}}\ @\mathrm{Co-NP}$ biohybrid $(15~\Omega)$ compared to the M. barkeri-CF $(425~\Omega)$ , which implies that the significantly enhanced extracellular electron transfer at the M. barkeri and ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ interface is mainly responsible for its outstanding methanogenesis performance. More information on electron transfer pathways at the biotic–abiotic interface is revealed in the cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests (Figure 2f and Figure S8, Supporting Information). Specifically, two relatively pronounced reduction peaks at around $-0.3$ and $-0.7\mathrm{~V~}$ are observed for M. barkeri-Co- $\mathbf{\cdotN}_{4}@\mathbf{Co-NP}$ biohybrid, while there was only one weak reduction peak at $-0.7\mathrm{~V~}$ for M. barkeri-CF. It has been revealed that the reduction peaks at round $-0.3$ and $-0.7\:\mathrm{V}$ are related to the direct electron transfer with Fe(III) meso-tetra(4- carboxyphenyl)porphine (Fe-TCPP) and mediated electron transfer with riboflavin, respectively.[32] Thus, the CV results suggest that the Co ${\bf-N}_{4}@{\mathsf{C o}}$ -NP bridge not only enhances the mediated electron transfer process but also builds up a direct electron transfer path. These two electron transfer paths are further elucidated by analyzing the corresponding conductive extracellular polymeric substance, as discussed in Section 2.3.
## 2.3. Biotic–Abiotic Electron Transfer Mechanisms within M. barkeri-Co- $N_{4}@{\mathsf{C o}}$ -NP Biohybrid
## 2.3.1. Direct Electron Transfer via Cytb
Cytb is a family of Fe-TCPP-containing proteins (e.g., EchF, EchB, EchA, HdrE, etc.), which work synergistically to enable electron transfer throughout the cell membrane.[16,38] The direct transmembrane electron transfer via Cytb is usually enabled by the intimate interaction between the Fe-TCPP and the electrode matrix. Yet, there is no signal of interaction for Cytb (Peak C) extracted from the control biohybrid of M. barkeri-CF (Figures S10d and S11, Supporting Information), indicating that its direct electron transfer path is interrupted. This is further evidenced by the large adsorption energy barrier of Cytb on the CF $(+0.11\mathrm{eV}_{\mathrm{:}}$ Figure 3d). In sharp contrast, there is no energy barrier for Cytb adsorbed on ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-based nanosheets $(-0.10\mathrm{eV})$ . The intensive peak C together with the fluorescence peak of Cytb (Figure 3a and Figure S10b, Supporting Information) further implies the strong charge communication between Fe-TCPP and ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ during the direct electron transfer process. Besides, the pre-edge of Fe in Cytb exhibits an obvious shift from iron (II) phthalocyanine (Fe-Pc, representing $\mathrm{Fe-N_{4}}$ structure) to Fe foil (Figure 3c and Figure S12, Supporting Information) when the microbes are absorbed on the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ electrode, suggesting a reduced average valence state of the Fe species due to charge injection. To confirm the electron transfer from Co${\mathrm{N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ to Fe-TCPP in Cytb, Fe-TCPP was further directly electrodeposited on the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ electrode for XPS measurements (Figure S13, Supporting Information). Based on the peak shift of Co- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}}$ to higher energy and that of Fe to lower energy in their XPS spectra (Figure S13, Supporting Information),[39] an electron flow from ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ to Fe-TCPP is thus clearly portrayed. In addition, strong fluorescence intensities of Cytb are also found in the LB-EPS and TB-EPS extracted from M. barkeri-Co- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}@C o-N P}$ biohybrid (Figures S9b,c and S10a,b, Supporting Information), suggesting that Cytb forms a close and unobstructed electrontransfer chain within the three conjoint EPS layers for efficient direct electron transfer.
As Co- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}}$ has been revealed as the interaction site in direct electron transfer, the function of the underneath Co nanoparticles in this electron path needs further exploration. It is first observed that the introduction of Co nanoparticles largely strengthens the adsorption of Cytb on the nanosheet-based electrode as the absorption energy of Cytb decreased from $-0.03\ \mathrm{eV}$ for ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}$ to $-0.10\mathrm{eV}$ for ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge (Figure 3d). The charge density differences were further calculated when Fe-TCPP as the central molecular of Cytb was absorbed on ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}.$ and Co $\cdot\mathrm{N}_{4}@\mathrm{Co}.$ NP bridge-based nanosheets (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Compared to the bare $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ , the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge greatly enlarges the redistributed electronic cloud as a large number of electrons are transferred from Co nanoparticles to FeTCPP through ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ sites, indicating enhanced electric communication between Fe-TCPP and the nanosheet electrode. This reinforced electric interaction is also evidenced by the increased total density of states (TDOS) at the Fermi energy level $(E_{\mathrm{f}})$ of the model pair consisting of Fe-TCPP and ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ compared to that of the control model pair of Fe-TCPP and $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ (Figure 3e). The TDOS center $(\epsilon)$ shifts from $-5.84\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ -based pair model to $-2.99\mathrm{eV}$ for the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ -based pair model. However, the project density of states (PDOS) shows that the d-band centers of Co orbital shift oppositely to that of the TDOS centers, i.e., from $-1.68\mathrm{eV}$ for the ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ -based model pair to $-1.88\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathbf{Co-N_{4}@C o-N l}$ P-based model pair. This implies that Co mainly plays the role of electronic structure tailoring in the hybrid rather than serving as the direct charge donator in the interaction with Fe-TCPP.
According to the concept of quantum chemistry, the valence electron energy of an element plays a vital role in building an electric interaction between different elements.[40] For metal elements, the valence electrons are d-band electrons, while for nonmetal elements are p-band electrons. Therefore, a more detailed charge communication path between Fe-TCPP and $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ active sites is analyzed with the $d\cdot p$ model based on the theoretical model of the d-band center.[41] According to the d-band center of Fe and p-band centers of $\mathrm{~N~}$ and C in Fe-TCPP, as well as the d-band center of Co and p-band centers of $\mathrm{~N~}$ and C in the nanosheets-based electrode (Figure 3e and Figure S15, Supporting Information), the difference between the d-band center and the p-band center $(\Delta d-p)$ for different element combination are calculated to reflect the electron transfer barriers between them. A lower value of $(\Delta d-p)$ represents a smaller energy band gap and thus corresponds to a facilitated electron transfer trend. As depicted in Figure 3g, Fe-N with the smallest $(\Delta d-p)$ demonstrates the most likely charge transfer direction, i.e., from the N element in ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ to the Fe element in Fe-TCPP.[40,41] This preference is in agreement with the charge density differences study where an electron-deficient metal (Co) and electron-rich nitrogen-doped carbons are observed in atomic metal-doped carbon nanosheets.[23,42] Besides, the $(\Delta d-p)$ of N-Fe for $\mathrm{Co-N}_{4}@\mathrm{Co}.$ NP decreases to ${\approx}2.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ from ${\approx}3.8\mathrm{eV}$ for that of ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ , indicating that the Co nanoparticles in the $\mathbf{Co-N_{4}@C o-N P}$ bridge effectively fill up the electronic states gap between ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}$ and Fe-TCCP via tailoring the electronic structure of the ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ sites. This decreased energy band gap further reduces the charge transfer barrier between Fe-TCCP and the ${\mathsf{C o}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ active sites, which in turn strengthens the direct transmembrane electron transfer.
## 2.3.2. Mediated Electron Transfer via Redox Mediators in Coenzyme F420
Coenzyme $F_{420}$ has been revealed to be the protein carrier for conducting mediated electron transfer, during which its central molecular of riboflavin works as the redox mediator. Different from the demanding direct electron transfer path via Cytb, mediated electron transfer via coenzyme $F_{420}$ is observed for both $M$ barkeri-Co- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}@C o-N P}$ and $M$ . barkeri-CF biohybrids in the CV tests (Figure 2f). The intrinsic reason for the low enforcement barrier of mediated electron transfer process lies in the strong adsorption of coenzyme $F_{420}$ on the electrodes (Figure 3d). Particularly, compare to that on CF $(-0.39\ \mathrm{eV})$ , the adsorption energy of coenzyme $F_{420}$ on ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ further decreases to $-0.57\mathrm{eV}.$ This stronger adsorption force is supposed to facilitate the charge communication between $F_{420}$ and the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ as verified by the larger redox capacity of $F_{420}$ on $\mathrm{Co-N}_{4}@\mathrm{Co}.$ NP (Figure S16, Supporting Information). Riboflavin was directly used to represent coenzyme $F_{420}$ in investigating the redox activity via CV tests. Specifically, an identical amount of riboflavin was employed in the measurements to rule out the influence of the $F_{420}$ enrichment difference. The much larger redox peak current density with ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ than that with CF suggests a facilitated charge communication enabled by a higher redox activity of riboflavin on the $\mathbf{Co-N_{4}@C o-N P}$ electrode. In addition to the redox activity, the abundance of coenzyme $F_{420}$ on $\mathbf{Co-N_{4}@C o-N P}$ , which is reflected by its fluorescence intensity (Figure 3a,b and Figure S11, Supporting Information), exhibits a 10.5-fold increase compared to that on CF. These together well confirm that the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge significantly facilitates the mediated electron transfer at the biotic–abiotic interface.
It is worth noting that electrons are accompanied by protons in coenzyme $F_{420}$ -mediated electron transfer (Figure S17, Supporting Information). Specifically, the redox mediator of riboflavin in coenzyme $F_{420}$ accomplishes the reduction process by capturing electrons and protons outside the microbes with its two $-C=N-$ moieties (denoted hydrogenation of riboflavin). The hydrogenated riboflavin further undergoes the oxidization process inside the microbes and releases electrons and protons for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion (denoted dehydrogenation of the hydrogenated riboflavin). To further reveal the mechanisms of ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-enhanced mediated electron transfer, the electron and proton capture processes of riboflavin are investigated in detail via density functional theory calculation. The large gain and loss of electrons indicate the bonding of the $-C=N-$ group in riboflavin with ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ sites in ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ , and the Co nanoparticles further enhance their electron migration flux due to its electronic structure tuning effect as discussed before (Figure S18, Supporting Information). This is in agreement with the shift of the energy level center toward the $E_{\mathrm{f}}$ in TDOS analysis of the model pair of riboflavin and ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ (Figure 3f). Similarly, the difference $(\Delta d-p)$ between the d-band center of the Co element in ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}$ and the p-band center of $\mathrm{~N~}$ and C elements in riboflavin was calculated to reflect the electron transfer barriers between them according to the $d\cdot p$ model. Notably, a lower $(\Delta d-p)$ value is obtained with Co-N for $\mathbf{Co-N_{4}@C o-N P}$ while that was $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-C}$ for ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}$ (Figure 3h and Figure S19, Supporting Information), suggesting that the Co nanoparticles change the electron flow pathway from $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-C}$ to $\mathrm{Co-N}$ . As depicted in Figure S17 in the Supporting Information, the N site in the $-C=N-$ moiety of riboflavin is the hydrogenation site during the mediated electron transfer process. Thus, the direct electron flow to the hydrogenation site in the riboflavin further promotes the hydrogenation of riboflavin, as evidenced by the lowered energy barrier in this process (Figure 3i and Figure S20, Supporting Information).
## 2.3.3. Potential-Dependent Charge Transfer Paths
To further investigate the electron transfer process at the biotic– abiotic interface under different applied potentials, in situ Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was carried out to record the characteristic peak intensity of the conductive proteins for charge transfer. First, the expression of the genes involved in the electron transfer process for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to- $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ conversion was obtained using quantitative reverse transcriptionpolymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) via editing the conductive member proteins (Figure S21, Supporting Information). EchAB and MP are identified as the major direct electron receiver protein clusters in Cytb. Meantime, Fpo and HdrABC are recognized as the mediated electron transfer protein clusters in coenzyme $F_{420}$ .[18] It is found that the expressions of these conductive proteins for M. barkeri-Co- ${\bf\cdot N}_{4}@{\bf C o-N P}$ biohybrid increase significantly compared to that for the control biohybrid of M. barkeriCF, following its enhanced charge transfer. Then, the stretching vibrations of the -CH- (located at $1362.5\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and -NH- (located at $1575.2\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) bonds in the protein centers of EchAB and MP were characterized, respectively, to reflect the direct electron transfer under different applied potentials.[43–45] As depicted in Figure 3j, the sum of the characteristic peak intensities of -CHand -NH- demonstrate a sharp increase starting from $-0.2{\mathrm{~V}},$ which represents the potential required to overcome the electric field-induced polarization for electron release.[46–48] With further decreased potential, their peak width and intensity demonstrate a trend of first increasing and then decreasing. The strongest peak intensity is observed at a potential between $-0.4$ and $-0.7\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 3k), suggesting that the direct electron transfer is most active in this potential range. Similarly, the stretching vibration of the -COOH (located at $1787.5\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) in the protein center of Fpo and HdrABC was tracked to investigate the potential dependence of mediated electron transfer.[49] Particularly, the peak intensity of -COOH first increases progressively between 0 and $-0.4\:\mathrm{V},$ and then remains relatively unchanged with further decreased potential (Figure 3l), implying that the efficiency of mediated electron transfer is quite stable after it gets over the corresponding charge transfer barrier. This well explains that the FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ obtained with M. barkeri-CF holds relatively steady at the tested potentials, while that obtained with M. barkeri-Co $\mathsf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathrm{-N}_{4}@\mathsf{C o}\mathrm{-NP}$ exhibits a slightly decreased FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at large potentials (Figure 2c).
## 2.4. Enhanced Intracellular Methanogenesis in M. barkeri-Co- $N_{4}@{\mathsf{C o}}$ -NP Biohybrid
It is worth noting that a higher expression of the energy conversion protein (i.e., ATPase) is observed for M. barkeri adsorbed on the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ electrode than that on the CF electrode during the quantitative RT-PCR measurement, demonstrating a 1.8- fold increase (Figure S21, Supporting Information). The ATPase, which catalyzes the generation of ATP with ADP and phosphate, serves as the driving engine of the cells for metabolic activities.[16] Therefore, the higher level of ATPase corresponds to a more active intracellular metabolism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to- $\mathrm{.CH_{4}}$ conversion. As the driven force for ATP synthesis by ATPase is the inter/extracellular $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ concentration field, SEM-EDS was carried out to characterize the $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ concentration at the surface of the biohybrids (Figure 4f,i,k, and Figure S22, Supporting Information). As expected, densely and evenly distributed Na element is observed on the M. barkeri-Co- $\mathrm{N}_{4}@\mathsf{C o-N P}$ biohybrid while only a trace amount of Na is detected on the M. barkeri-CF biohybrid. Generally, ion diffusion toward the electrode surface is mainly affected by the gradient field of concentration, potential, and thermal. Suppose that there is no distinct influence from the thermal and concentration field in the system at the beginning of the electrochemical tests, the potential field will be the dominator factor for ion transfer in the system. As such, the electric field around the electrodes was simulated via FEA to demonstrate its effect on manipulating intracellular metabolism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion by tailoring the $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ distribution at the interface of the biohybrid and the electrolyte.
For the CF electrode without the construction of nanostructures, its surface can be roughly taken as a relatively smooth plane in FEA simulation. As for the ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-based nanosheets on the CF matrix, an average sheet thickness of $200~\mathrm{nm}$ and a space between two joint nanosheets of $250~\mathrm{nm}$ were adopted for simulation according to their SEM images (Figure 4a,b). As shown in Figure 4c, a stronger electric field with boosted electric intensity at the tip is observed for the nanosheets compared to bare CF. The larger electric intensity suggests that the interior surface of the electrode is more negatively charged.
This, therefore, provides a stronger force to attract cations like $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ at the outer surface to maintain the charge balance, leading to a higher current density of $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ flow (Figure 4e–h). Consequently, significantly facilitated $\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ accumulation is demonstrated at the surface of the nanosheet-based electrode, in terms of both speed and amount (Figure 4d,j). Besides, compared to bare CF (Figure $^{4\mathrm{g})}$ , the concentration boundary line of $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ moves up accordingly at the surface of the nanosheet-based electrode, suggesting sufficient $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ filled up the spaces of the nanosheet arrays (Figure 4d) to drive the ATPase-catalyzed energy conversion process.[50] Particularly, the $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ concertation obtained with simulation correlated well with that obtained from the experimental measurements (Figure 4l), which was measured with EDS mapping. The higher value of the $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ concertation at the surface of the nanosheet-based electrode thus firmly proves the enhanced ATP synthesis in M. barkeri-Co- $\cdot\mathrm{N}_{4}@\mathrm{Co}.$ NP biohybrid, which further supports the active intracellular metabolism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{to}{\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ conversion. Thus, the synergistic effect of ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co-NP}}$ bridge-enhanced biotic–abiotic electron transfer and intracellular metabolism (Figure S23, Supporting Information) result in a record high methane production rate in this work (Figure 2h and Table S4, Supporting Information).
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, this work carefully interrogates the biotic–abiotic interface of the highly efficient M. barkeri- ${\mathsf{C o-N}}_{4}@{\mathsf{C o-N P}}$ biohybrid in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to- $\mathrm{.CH_{4}}$ conversion with an extremely high methane production rate of $3860\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{day}^{-1}$ $[-1.0\mathrm{V}$ vs $\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}_{i}^{\prime}$ and a Faradaic efficiency of $85\%$ . The excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion performance of the M. barkeri-Co- $\mathrm{\bfN}_{4}@\mathrm{Co-NP}$ biohybrid is attributed to the coupled effect of facilitated biotic–abiotic electron transfer together with enhanced intracellular methanogenesis. The atomic dispersed ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-N}}_{4}$ sites render a fast direct electron transfer pathway via directly bonding with the molecular center of Cytb. The Co nanoparticles simultaneously facilitate this direct electron transfer pathway and the $F_{420}$ -mediated electron transfer pathway due to their multiorbital tuning operations on surface electronic structures. Besides, the potential dependence of these electron transfer paths is also uncovered through in situ FTIR. Notably, the nanosheet-based structure and the tuning effect of the crafted $\mathbf{Co-N_{4}@C o-N P}$ bridge in the nanosheet significantly enhance the surface electric field of the electrode, which further promotes interfacial $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ accumulation and results in a higher local $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ concentration to power the ATPase transport, leading to increased ATP synthesis for intracellular $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion. The fundamental scrutinization of the biotic–abiotic interface rendered by the atomic-nanoparticle bridge-based electrode demonstrates a broadened correlation between the abiotic electrode matrix and the attached microorganisms including both electric and metabolic aspects, therefore providing a solid step for further optimization of the bio-electrochemical systems.
